1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,975
The Lane Was Probably Named After My School.

2
00:00:02,975 --> 00:00:04,975
Neither Is There Anymore.

3
00:00:04,975 --> 00:00:11,250
I was born in 1960 on Jalan Payoh Lai, in what was then the Kangkar area of Hougang.

4
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:20,200
Memory that old gets slippery, so I can't be certain of every detail, but I remember walking to school and to church there for years afterward.

5
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:24,775
The walk from the Upper Serangoon Road junction took about twenty minutes.

6
00:00:24,775 --> 00:00:31,400
My favourite shortcut ran along a backlane to the left of Montfort School called Holy Innocents' Lane.

7
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:42,050
I only learned recently, while researching this post, that the name traces back to a cluster of Catholic schools that once shared that stretch of Upper Serangoon Road.

8
00:00:42,050 --> 00:00:44,075
Montfort was one of them.

9
00:00:44,075 --> 00:00:49,825
Today, neither the lane nor the cemetery that once stood beside it appears on any map.

10
00:00:49,825 --> 00:00:55,175
Sundays meant the same walk in the same direction, except to Mass instead of school.

11
00:00:55,175 --> 00:00:59,175
I'd cut through whatever backlane shaved the most time off the trip.

12
00:00:59,175 --> 00:01:09,300
The church at the end of it, Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary, was older than anything else I knew as a child, though I had no idea how much older at the time.

13
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:19,125
A French missionary priest, Fr. Ambroise Maistre, bought the land in 1857 for the Teochew Catholic farmers and fishermen of Aukang.

14
00:01:19,125 --> 00:01:22,350
Many of the converts came from Shantou in China.

15
00:01:22,350 --> 00:01:33,825
The original attap chapel eventually gave way to the Neo-Gothic building that still stands today, blessed in December 1901 and expanded again in 1933.

16
00:01:33,825 --> 00:01:37,425
It has been a national monument since 2005.

17
00:01:37,425 --> 00:01:50,300
It still holds a Teochew-language Mass too, a tradition instituted in 1971 that is, as far as anyone can confirm, the last regular Teochew Catholic Mass left in Singapore.

18
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:54,800
Montfort School, next door, turned out to be part of that same tangle.

19
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:04,475
It was founded in 1916, not as Montfort but as Holy Innocents' English School, established beside the church by the parish priest of the time.

20
00:02:04,475 --> 00:02:08,700
The Brothers of St Gabriel took it over in 1936.

21
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:14,200
It wasn't the only "Holy Innocents" on that stretch of Upper Serangoon Road, though.

22
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:21,875
Holy Innocents' Boys School grew out of the same church grounds, and so did what's now CHIJ Our Lady of the Nativity.

23
00:02:21,875 --> 00:02:29,050
Holy Innocents' High School traces back further still, to 1892, and remains a separate school today.

24
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:42,500
The names collided so often that in 1958 Holy Innocents' English School was renamed Montfort, after St Louis Marie Grignion de Montfort, the order's founder, specifically to end the confusion.

25
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:48,125
I can't say for certain which of those schools Holy Innocents' Lane was actually named for.

26
00:02:48,125 --> 00:02:52,900
Montfort, my own school, was simply one of at least three that could have claimed it.

27
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:57,350
Montfort itself moved to Hougang Avenue 8 in 1992.

28
00:02:57,350 --> 00:03:03,050
At some point after that, the lane and the cemetery next to it were cleared away entirely.

29
00:03:03,050 --> 00:03:09,100
"Hougang" is the Mandarin reading of the Hokkien and Teochew "Au Kang," meaning the end of the river.

30
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:11,850
Kangkar itself meant "foot of the stream."

31
00:03:11,850 --> 00:03:21,375
That's where the settlements clustered, wherever Teochew planters ran their kangchu system of jungle-clearing plantations along Sungei Serangoon.

32
00:03:21,375 --> 00:03:27,575
There were roughly twenty such kangkars across the island by an 1849 newspaper count.

33
00:03:27,575 --> 00:03:36,125
By 1986, more than nine in ten Kangkar villagers were still Teochew, and the dialect was still the area's everyday language.

34
00:03:36,125 --> 00:03:40,200
The Kangkar I half-remember was cleared in 1984.

35
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:48,425
Its families were resettled into three- and four-room HDB flats, and its land was folded into what's now Punggol Park.

36
00:03:48,425 --> 00:03:57,600
Punggol End, further along, was a different kind of village entirely: Kampong Wak Sumang, a Malay fishing settlement at Tanjong Punggol.

37
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:02,125
By one descendant's account it was founded before 1819.

38
00:04:02,125 --> 00:04:12,300
It only got piped water, electricity and proper roads in the 1960s, and it became briefly well known afterward for its seafood restaurants and boatels.

39
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:19,425
The government announced reclamation in 1983 and told residents to vacate by 1986.

40
00:04:19,425 --> 00:04:29,550
The fishing port and wholesale market outlasted the kampong itself, but even that finally closed in 1997, to make way for Punggol New Town.

41
00:04:29,550 --> 00:04:38,650
Hougang New Town itself was announced in 1979, planned for 460 hectares and 120,000 residents.

42
00:04:38,650 --> 00:04:47,725
Its first neighbourhoods were finished by 1983, and the rest by 1992, the same year Montfort left its original site for good.

43
00:04:47,725 --> 00:04:52,525
One thing about the area never got redeveloped along with everything else.

44
00:04:52,525 --> 00:05:00,450
Hougang has been held by the same opposition party, the Workers' Party, in every general election since 1991.

45
00:05:00,450 --> 00:05:12,675
It's a small, quietly remarkable fact in its own right, less a story about any one politician than a marker of how far Singapore's electoral landscape has matured since the kampong days.

46
00:05:12,675 --> 00:05:17,825
I can't reconstruct the Kangkar of my childhood in any real detail anymore.

47
00:05:17,825 --> 00:05:25,425
The specific smells, the sounds, the neighbours' faces are mostly gone, the same way the lane and the cemetery are gone.

48
00:05:25,425 --> 00:05:36,875
What's left is the walk itself: twenty minutes each way, a shortcut past a school that used to have a different name, to a church that has outlasted every version of the neighbourhood around it.

49
00:05:36,875 --> 00:05:50,650
Why it matters today: Most of what made up the Hougang I grew up in — the lane, the cemetery, the kampong itself — exists now only in scattered memories like mine, and in records like the ones that let me write this.

50
00:05:50,650 --> 00:05:56,050
Montfort School and Nativity Church are the rare fixed points that survived the clearing.

51
00:05:56,050 --> 00:06:01,125
That's probably the only reason I can still find my way back to any of it at all.
